(2) VARIANCE SPLITS -- random error inflates SCATTER, not the mean
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  'predicted' below is the rms the Monte-Carlo budget says each half should have, given a common unexplained floor
  quality flag                   Q = 1 (high)             N= 75 rms=0.1529 | Q = 2 (medium)         N= 31 rms=0.1534   ratio 1.00 (predicted 0.89), Levene p = 0.552
  distance method                Hubble flow              N= 64 rms=0.1515 | direct                 N= 42 rms=0.1524   ratio 0.99 (predicted 1.36), Levene p = 0.705
  inclination                    i >= median              N= 54 rms=0.1585 | i < median             N= 52 rms=0.1363   ratio 1.16 (predicted 0.85), Levene p = 0.263
  distance error                 e_D/D above median       N= 53 rms=0.1464 | below median           N= 53 rms=0.1599   ratio 0.92 (predicted 1.40), Levene p = 0.747
  angular size of 2 kpc          well resolved            N= 69 rms=0.1568 | poorly resolved        N= 37 rms=0.1464   ratio 1.07 (predicted 0.85), Levene p = 0.882
  the budget's own sigma         predicted sigma high     N= 53 rms=0.1480 | predicted sigma low    N= 53 rms=0.1565   ratio 0.95 (predicted 1.68), Levene p = 0.422
  the last split is the sharp one: it divides the sample by the budget's OWN predicted per-galaxy sigma, so it is where
  the budget makes its strongest prediction -- variance ratio 2.83 predicted, 0.89 observed, and the F test
  at 53 vs 53 galaxies resolves anything above 1.73.  Under the budget's model the observed ratio has p = 2.9e-05.
  [PASS] 116E AGAINST MY OWN ESTIMATOR -- the budget's TOTAL is right (check 116F) but its PER-GALAXY ALLOCATION is refuted.  Split by the budget's own predicted sigma, it demands a variance ratio near 2.8 between the halves; the data give 0.9, in the opposite direction, and this is the one split an F test on 53 galaxies a side can actually resolve.  The most likely culprit is SPARC's Hubble-flow distance allowances -- a blanket 14-30% covering peculiar-velocity systematics, treated here as Gaussian per-galaxy errors, which over-assigns scatter to exactly the galaxies carrying the largest quoted e_D   (predicted variance ratio 2.83 (resolvable threshold 1.73), observed 0.89, p = 2.9e-05; the other five splits are all within noise (largest observed rms ratio 1.16, smallest Levene p 0.263) and none of them is resolvable)
  Consequence: the 81% observational share below is a match of TOTALS, not a demonstration that the residual is
  distributed the way the quoted errors say it is.  Quote it with that caveat attached.

