====================================================================================================================
VERDICT -- item 116
====================================================================================================================
  The framework's wish is GRANTED by the budget and NOT by the correlations, and the two are not in conflict.

  Granted, in total: an end-to-end Monte Carlo of SPARC's own quoted errors -- distance, inclination, velocity --
  plus the 0.11 dex stellar-population scatter in Upsilon reproduces 0.139 of the observed 0.154 rms, i.e. 81% of
  the variance, with nothing fitted.  The largest single contributor is the distance error acting through a FIXED
  PHYSICAL 2 kpc aperture, which is a feature of item 23's estimator and not of the framework.

  Not granted, twice.  (i) No single predictor correlates significantly once the look-elsewhere effect over sixteen
  predictors is paid for (global p = 0.10).  The strongest correlate is a systematic (inclination, rho = -0.26) and
  the strongest PHYSICAL ones -- gas fraction and mass -- were the assumed BULGE mass-to-light ratio in disguise:
  the bulged/bulgeless offset nulls at Upsilon_b = 0.52 and every physical correlation dissolves on the bulgeless
  subsample.  That is the hunt's bug pattern 5, caught again.  (ii) More seriously, and against my own estimator:
  the budget's per-galaxy ALLOCATION is refuted.  Split by the budget's own predicted sigma it demands a variance
  ratio near 2.8 between the halves; the data give 0.9, in the opposite direction, and that split is the one an F
  test on this sample can resolve.  So the 81% is an agreement of TOTALS.  The honest reading is that SPARC's
  blanket Hubble-flow distance allowances (14-30%, one value per method) are not Gaussian per-galaxy errors.

  Left over: about 0.066 in the ratio, roughly 10% in v(2 kpc), unexplained.  It is NOT an a_0 effect -- the two
  footings give the same scatter to 0.2%, so item 23's residual is useless as an a_0 diagnostic -- and the leading
  suspect is that item 23 uses the SPHERICAL kernel relation on a DISC.  Closing that gap needs the repository's
  own AQUAL/QUMOND disc solve, not another correlation.

